        };
    ;
swRun();
print "STORET SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

storeT -- THE WRITERS CENSUS, ONE ROW PER WRITER.

Tony ruled 2026-09-05 that AN ARMED STATEMENT STORES NOTHING. opAssign was the
first writer taught to consult the arm; this file is the census that found the
other thirteen and the certificate that each one consults it.

EVERY ROW IS THE SAME SHAPE, deliberately: a target holding a DISTINCT starting
value, and one statement whose right-hand side refuses before the write. The
target must come back UNCHANGED.

⚠ THE STARTING VALUES ARE ALL DIFFERENT (101..111) AND THAT IS NOT DECORATION.
A row wanting 0, or wanting the same number as its neighbour, could be satisfied
by a field that was never written, or by a write that landed on the wrong field.
A distinct non-zero value can only be there because THAT field was left alone.

⚠ THE REFUSAL COMES FROM `+*` ON A SUBSCRIPT MISS, not from a star. Once
operand-producing operators refuse terminally, a star in front would arm FIRST
and the row could no longer say which writer was being tested. Grokking["swNoRule"]
is a genuine null with no refusal ahead of it.

⚠⚠ TWO WRITERS ARE NOT CERTIFIED HERE AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL, NOT
LAZINESS. `++` and `--` are UNARY: there is nothing to their left in a statement
that could arm before they write, so a single statement cannot both raise a
refusal and reach them. They carry the consult -- it is in the source -- but
this file cannot exercise it, and saying so is better than a row that looks like
a test and is not.

ONE stop(), sentinel at the foot.
